# Connectivity and authentication decision

## Status

Revised on 2026-09-30 after reviewing Proton's current Mail session SDK,
WebClients desktop-fork implementation, and the official Drive SDK third-party
login flow.

## Decision

The primary version-one path is Proton Mail's ordinary web application in a
dedicated project browser profile. The user performs login visibly through
Proton's own UI; automation begins only after the expected authenticated Mail
application state is present. Proton Mail Bridge is not a product dependency or
fallback.

The web adapter never attaches to an existing personal browser profile and never
automates password, second-factor, CAPTCHA, recovery, or security-key input. It
does not export cookies or web storage into MCP, config files, fixtures, or
logs. Session custody remains inside the dedicated profile owned by the user.

The first implemented slice is `protonmail-ai auth login`. It resolves an
absolute XDG data home, falls back to `$HOME/.local/share`, creates only the
project-owned `protonmail-ai/browser-profile`, restricts the project/profile
directories to `0700` on Linux, and rejects symlinked project/profile paths.
The Chromium launch uses an explicit `--user-data-dir` and no remote-debugging
flag. Login completion detection and mailbox driving remain separate unfinished
adapter work.

Before any future browser interaction, the web adapter requires semantic
`UiEvidence` and an explicit `UiTarget`. The policy cross-checks provider state,
capability, side-effect class, action grant, and expected mailbox/composer
surface. Login, authentication challenges, unknown blockers, and page-shape
drift authorize no target.

Managed automation additionally requires exclusive ownership of the dedicated
profile. `AutomationProfileLease` parses Chromium's `SingletonLock` and
refuses a live local Chrome-family owner, foreign host, or malformed lock; it
then creates a project-local `0600` lease containing only PID and Linux process
start ticks. A second live owner fails closed; a dead owner can be reclaimed.
The lease records inode/device identity before cleanup so stale recovery never
deletes a replacement file.

Chromium's singleton files are treated as provider state and are never removed
by protonmail-ai.

Managed browser control uses Chromium `--remote-debugging-pipe` only on the
non-default project profile. The parent maps the private protocol to child file
descriptors 3 and 4; no remote-debugging TCP port is opened. The transport caps
one response at 1 MiB and uses five-second pipe I/O timeouts. It also bounds
unsolicited events while waiting for a response.

Signed-in readiness is origin-bound. Discovery accepts exactly one Proton
Mail/Account page and then re-checks its execution context using only
`location.protocol`, `location.hostname`, and `location.port`. Target metadata
and live context must agree. `account.proton.me` remains human-owned
login/challenge state.

For HTTPS `mail.proton.me`, shell inspection obtains only the DOM root identity
and issues role-filtered accessibility queries for `navigation`, `search`,
`dialog`, and `alertdialog`. It does not request the full accessibility tree or
retain accessible names. Navigation and search must both have an unignored AX
node, while either dialog role blocks readiness. `require_mail_shell` turns
missing or blocked shell evidence into a typed fail-closed snapshot.

That snapshot is not an action grant. A mailbox workflow must attach and repeat
its origin/page-state validation in the execution path that reads content or
creates a side effect, so navigation after inspection cannot authorize stale
state.

The first content-bearing read follows that rule in one attached target session:
origin and Mail shell are checked, content-free list evidence is captured, only
visible row metadata is projected, list evidence is captured again, and origin
is rechecked. Before/after list evidence and opaque row IDs must match exactly
in rendered order.

Visible-row projection reads only the row's provider ID, subject title,
displayed correspondent-address title, and unread class. It does not claim
message-versus-conversation semantics and does not read body content. Loading,
skeletons, duplicate IDs, explicit contradictions, unproven zero-row state, or
a changing list invalidate the snapshot. An empty snapshot is returned only
when Mail renders its explicit empty marker on both observations.

A separate mode proof handles only locations where WebClients forces message
mode independent of the user's Conversation grouping setting. The pinned
`isConversationMode` rule disables grouping for Drafts, All drafts, Sent, All
sent, Deleted, and active searches. The adapter evaluates those same route/hash
rules inside the page and returns only `forcedMessageRoute` and `activeSearch`
booleans over CDP; raw pathname/hash/search text never crosses that boundary.

`read_visible_message_page` requires `Messages` mode before row projection and
re-checks it after the stable snapshot. A mode change invalidates the result.
All other locations remain `Unknown`; in particular Inbox is never guessed to
be messages or conversations. The localized screen-reader heading does reflect
conversation mode upstream, but localized accessible text is deliberately not
a production discriminator.

Read-only message pagination is a bounded UI-navigation action, not mailbox
mutation. The adapter reads a stable proven-message snapshot first, requires an
explicit current page and enabled Next state, then activates exactly one
`pagination-row:go-to-next-page` button. It waits for the provider list to
settle on exactly `N+1` while rechecking origin and message mode, then takes a
fresh
stable snapshot. Missing/disabled/replaced controls, page jumps, origin drift,
and mode drift fail closed.

`MailboxListPagination.tsx` at that same WebClients main commit is
byte-identical to the pinned snapshot. Its blob is
`4f8ffb454bb9769192e48ae0dcba72477f1d317d`.
Its Next button is disabled while loading or on the final page. The surrounding
container always renders either `message-list-loading` or `message-list-loaded`,
so a missing list root during the bounded transition is compatibility failure,
not silently tolerated loading state.

### Provider-neutral list-message readiness

Visible sort state is inspected through `filter-dropdown:show-filters` and the
four stable `toolbar:sort-*` test IDs. `aria-pressed` is reduced to one typed
`MailboxSortOrder`; no translated label or private Redux/store state is read.
If the adapter opens the menu solely to inspect it, it closes the menu before
returning, including on contradictory sort evidence.

Origin is revalidated immediately before both open and cleanup clicks, and drift
blocks interaction on the new page. It never activates a sort option.

A semantic newest-first selection does not satisfy the frozen `list_messages`
ordering/cursor contract by itself; numeric `Message.Time` is now reconciled to
stable visible IDs, but current WebClients can map the visible time controls to
`SnoozeTime` or reverse `Time` direction by location. The bounded Network
observer therefore supplies sanitized provider `Sort` and `Desc` evidence to
readiness: exact `Sort=Time&Desc=1` removes `ProviderSortKeyUnproven`, while
`SnoozeTime`, ascending `Time`, or another provider sort retains it and
non-newest visible UI state additionally reports `SortNotNewestFirst`.
`ProviderTieBreakDiffers` and `MissingSnapshotBoundary` remain unconditional, so
public `list_messages` is still unavailable.


The list/row source files and the mode-rule files were rechecked against
WebClients `main@4ceca26f3fe59fbf8bcb1b97fe76b298b7c6a586` on 2026-10-01.
`mailSettings.ts`, `location.ts`, `elementSearch.ts`, `mailboxUrl.ts`,
`MailboxListScreenReaderHeading.tsx`, and `MailboxListContainer.tsx` are
byte-identical to the pinned snapshot, alongside the previously verified
list/row evidence. This is compatibility evidence, not
a promise that private UI contracts are permanent.

The pinned WebClients source supports those structural facts: Mail renders its
sidebar with `SidebarNav`, whose shared component is a `nav` landmark, and its
search wrapper declares `role="search"`. No localized label is part of the
adapter contract. Foreign/spoofed hosts, non-default ports, malformed AX
responses, and origin drift fail closed.

Chromium currently marks the Accessibility CDP domain as experimental, so
`queryAXTree` is a compatibility boundary rather than a permanent API promise.
A missing or changed command must surface as protocol incompatibility and
disable Mail readiness; the adapter never falls back silently to CSS selectors
or a full-tree scrape. Installed-Chrome smoke and live acceptance therefore
cover this boundary for supported releases.

The pinned WebClients tree also contains internal Lumo Mail read handlers such
as `open_folder` and `search`. Those handlers are built inside
`LumoMailProvider` from React hooks, Redux/store access, router history, and
other `MailToolDeps`; the snapshot does not declare them as a supported
browser-global integration surface. protonmail-ai therefore treats them as a
semantic research oracle only and does not reach into private React/Redux state
through CDP.

That oracle also shows why visible row count is insufficient as completion
evidence: its navigation reads wait for loading state, a context total, and
Encrypted Search completion before projecting rows. The external adapter must
find an observable equivalent before implementing mailbox reads; an empty
rendered list alone never proves the mailbox/view is empty.

Pinned WebClients evidence also provides a lower-risk compose handoff before the
full web adapter exists. Mail registers a `mailto:` protocol handler using the
`/inbox/#mailto=%s` path and accepts recipient, subject, Cc, Bcc, and body
fields. `protonmail-ai mail compose-url` implements this handoff without opening
a browser or creating a send side effect.

Direct session-fork access remains an optional protocol track. It is still
blocked on an authorized third-party Mail client identity; the SDK's unversioned
`Other` value is useful for modeling but is rejected for production bootstrap.
That blocker applies only to the optional direct adapter, not to the primary
Free-plan web path.

## Optional direct protocol evidence

This is not a reverse-engineered login shortcut. Proton's current Mail Rust SDK
contains the complete target and host QR-login state machine:

- the target calls `auth().from_fork().with_code()`;
- the target generates a 32-byte random encryption key;
- the QR payload is `0:user_code:base64_key:client_id`;
- the host encrypts the session key password for the target and pushes an
  independent fork;
- the target polls the fork, decrypts the payload, verifies that the user keys
  unlock, and completes the authenticated session.

The Mail SDK's session config derives the child client ID from its app version
and defaults to `Other`. That default documents the protocol shape, but the
production probe below shows it is not sufficient live authorization.

Proton's official Drive CLI independently demonstrates the same target-side
session-fork UX for third-party software: start a fork, present a Proton Account
handoff, poll for completion, store UID/access/refresh tokens and the encrypted
key password, and refresh the session without replaying account credentials.

Primary evidence:

- `ProtonMail/clients@2ecb794dbc221384dc6d88840965ac144db301ad`,
  `project/mail/rust/account/account-api/src/login/`
- `ProtonMail/clients@2ecb794dbc221384dc6d88840965ac144db301ad`,
  `project/mail/rust/api/mail-api-session/`
- `ProtonMail/clients@2ecb794dbc221384dc6d88840965ac144db301ad`,
  `project/mail/rust/mail/mail-common/examples/demo/cli/user/qr_login.rs`
- `ProtonDriveApps/sdk@f28a93ceaeb787283fe287e8c18b11e4cc7aad3a`,
  `incubating/account/js/src/auth.ts`
- `ProtonDriveApps/sdk@f28a93ceaeb787283fe287e8c18b11e4cc7aad3a`,
